00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:59.260
We don’t often get a chance to reference
the rest of the cosmos, so we’re going to seize

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.370
the moment while we can.

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:06.814
When the astronomer Carl Sagan narrated the
series “Cosmos,” he famously said the following:

00:01:07.281 --> 00:01:10.604
We are a way for the universe to know itself.

00:01:10.604 --> 00:01:14.950
Some part of our being knows this is where
we came from.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:16.806
We long to return.

00:01:16.806 --> 00:01:21.556
And we can, because the cosmos 
is also within us.

00:01:21.556 --> 00:01:24.105
We’re made of star stuff.

00:01:26.137 --> 00:01:31.780
Sagan was referring to the fact that our bodies
are built out of atoms that are billions of

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:38.500
years old, the pieces of stars that exploded
and scattered into space, eventually settling

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:43.180
into the matter that would form our earth
and then our bodies.

00:01:44.278 --> 00:01:49.240
But looking at these ciliates, it's tempting
to take Sagan’s words more literally, to

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:53.869
imagine that the little twinkles of light
are actually stars…

00:01:53.869 --> 00:02:00.560
…that we dived so deep into the microcosmos
that we have come full circle and stumbled

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.890
on the cosmos itself.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:09.280
In place of a telescope though, we have a microscope,
aimed at a million colorful constellations

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:14.777
enclosed into their own galaxies and separated
by membranes.

00:02:15.567 --> 00:02:21.250
But it takes more than just a microscope to
see the microcosmos lit up like this.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:26.989
James, our master of microcosmos, has to use
a tool called a polarizer, which restricts

00:02:26.989 --> 00:02:30.750
the vibration of light traveling towards our
samples.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:36.180
When this polarized light hits certain types
of materials, the result is what we see here:

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:37.518
shiny crystals.

00:02:38.277 --> 00:02:44.611
And as you might imagine, these intracellular
crystals have fascinated scientists for centuries.

00:02:44.611 --> 00:02:50.495
But the actual nature of these crystals—what
they are made of and what purpose they serve—

00:02:50.495 --> 00:02:53.630
that has been much murkier.

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:57.290
If you’ve been with us on our journey for
a while, you’ve probably heard us say the

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:00.646
phrase “we don’t know” a lot.

00:03:00.646 --> 00:03:05.860
The microcosmos does not guarantee answers,
and we have often found ourselves looking

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:12.480
at some unusual behavior or beautiful form
that represents some fascinating,

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.896
unresolved mystery.

00:03:14.896 --> 00:03:21.480
And this has happened almost every time we
have seen crystals in the microcosmos.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:28.770
We see them, we wonder what they are for,
and then we move on to say “we don’t know.”

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:33.739
But the great thing about science is that
“we don’t know” is often a precursor

00:03:33.739 --> 00:03:37.980
to people finding a way to learn more.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:43.489
In the summer of 2022, a group of scientists
published a paper in the International Society

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:49.009
for Microbial Ecology describing their 
investigation into the crystal structures

00:03:49.009 --> 00:03:52.503
found within single-celled eukaryotes.

00:03:52.503 --> 00:03:57.390
They used a technique called Raman microscopy,
which combines a standard microscope with

00:03:57.390 --> 00:04:02.700
other tools—including a laser—to excite
the molecules within a sample in ways that

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:10.813
scientists can effectively read in plots and
graphs that reveal the identity of those molecules.

00:04:10.813 --> 00:04:16.199
Every form of microscopy is a different way
of seeing the microcosmos.

00:04:16.199 --> 00:04:21.070
And for the scientists working on this paper,
Raman microscopy allowed them to search through

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:27.210
more than 200 species of eukaryotes and identify
the crystals within them.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:29.705
Now some of these crystals were previously known.

00:04:29.705 --> 00:04:33.729
Molecules like calcite and oxalate and carotenoids.

00:04:33.729 --> 00:04:38.690
And more than 80% of the organisms they studied
contained crystals made out of a type of compound

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:40.432
called purines.

00:04:40.432 --> 00:04:45.045
Purines are present in many types of compounds
that are important to life: 

00:04:45.045 --> 00:04:49.072
DNA, RNA, ATP…even caffeine.

00:04:49.072 --> 00:04:54.040
And in the case of these crystals, scientists
found purines appearing in different forms

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.780
like guanine and uric acid.

00:04:56.780 --> 00:05:02.660
For algae, the guanine crystals might function
as nitrogen storage, helping the organisms

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:07.104
weather the fluctuations in nitrogen available
to them in the environment.

00:05:07.104 --> 00:05:11.289
But there are also other types of crystals,
and other types of organisms that live in

00:05:11.289 --> 00:05:17.290
different habitats with different needs, which
means there are likely many other functions

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:20.110
that we still do not know about.

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:26.240
The ubiquity of purine crystals suggests that
these types of crystals might be one of the

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:34.066
oldest forms of biocrystallization, present
in the last common ancestor to all eukaryotes.

00:05:34.066 --> 00:05:37.155
So what does this mean for our organisms?

00:05:37.155 --> 00:05:39.177
Well… we don’t know.

00:05:39.177 --> 00:05:45.449
But identifying and characterizing these crystals
is just the first step on a longer journey,

00:05:45.449 --> 00:05:51.278
one that will reveal more when we learn how
to see what is right in front of us.

00:05:51.278 --> 00:05:56.070
And the more we learn about how these crystals
form and function in these organisms, the

00:05:56.070 --> 00:06:03.156
more we will understand about the story of life 
on earth, and even about how our own cells work.

00:06:03.156 --> 00:06:08.160
Because these crystals—removed as they are
from the original star stuff they are made

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:13.130
of—they still tie the cosmos and microcosmos
together.

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:16.690
They are beacons that do more than simply
sparkle.

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:18.430
They illuminate.

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:24.800
Microbes, they show us a way to know ourselves,
a part of our being that knows this is where

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:26.330
we came from.

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:31.030
There is no need to return 
because we are already there.

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:38.740
The microcosmos is within us, but more importantly,
we are in the microcosmos.

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:43.859
We are made of star stuff that has made the
same stuff: life.

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:57.000
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
